Sure! Here’s a quick snippet you can drop into your codebase.  
It assumes you already have a reference to the break node (`breakNode`) and that you want to change its label to `"loopEnd"` only when it isn’t already set to that value.

```js
// Your existing break node (replace this with whatever you actually have)
const breakNode = {
  type: 'BreakStatement',
  label: 'oldLabel',   // <-- could be anything or undefined
  // ... other properties ...
};

// Only update if the current label isn’t already 'loopEnd'
if (breakNode.label !== 'loopEnd') {
  // Pass the node as a stringified JSON object and the new label
  updateBreak({
    node: JSON.stringify(breakNode),
    label: 'loopEnd',
  });

  // After the call, `breakNode` will be replaced/updated internally
}
```

**What happens under the hood**

* `updateBreak` receives the node as a string (so it can safely serialize/deserialize it).
* It checks whether the node’s current label matches the supplied one.
* If they differ, it creates a new break node with the desired label and replaces the old node in place.

Feel free to adjust the surrounding logic to fit your AST traversal or manipulation framework. Happy coding!
